nodecode-laso is a rewrite of LasoFinance's LasoFinance/hermes-plugin:
https://github.com/LasoFinance/hermes-plugin
commit 8a046544307af690f04277bcb04929e5d1d58970
MIT, Copyright (c) Laso Finance (declared in plugin.yaml, author "Laso Finance"; the
repository carries no LICENSE file)

What was rewritten: the plugin's 66 MCP tools are here as two tools over one
catalog — `laso_tools` searches the names, titles and approval flags lifted from
the plugin's generated tools.json, and `laso_call` forwards one JSON-RPC
`tools/call` to https://laso.finance/mcp with the same `laso_` prefix dropped from
the name. The plugin's `pre_tool_call` hook, which asked the human to approve the 19
calls that move money or mint a credential, is here the `confirm` argument those 19
refuse without; its message was the same scalar summary, and the same caveat holds
— a nested argument such as a recipient object is not shown in the prompt. The
OAuth device flow of sign_in.py is ported as it stood (the same endpoints, the same
client metadata document, the same poll interval behaviour and the same "call again
to finish" step), with the key kept in this home's own file instead of a Hermes env
file. The plugin's system-prompt instructions are the primer `(help :laso)` answers.
Python's urllib became one `nlk:http` exchange per call.

What was left out, and why: `plugin.yaml`, which is Hermes's own manifest (this
folder's `.asd` is the manifest Nodecode reads); the plugin's unit tests, which
tested Python plumbing rather than the API; the `skills/workflows/SKILL.md` file, a
skill in Hermes's skill format — its guidance is the same primer's prose, and
Nodecode's skill format is a different one; and the per-tool JSON schemas, which
live on the server: a call that names an argument wrongly is told so by Laso. Also
left out: reading the key from Hermes's env file and its dashboard, since a cell
keeps its key in its own home.
